---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';

const portals = [
  { name: 'Access', text: 'One front door where members, contractors, and union staff sign in to the right experience.' },
  { name: 'Member app', text: 'A mobile-first app for rank-and-file laborers: dispatch, dues, training, benefits, and digital credentials in their pocket.' },
  { name: 'Contractor portal', text: 'Where contractors request workers and manage the paperwork that follows.' },
  { name: 'Union operations', text: 'The desktop workspace for business agents, dispatchers, and officers, and the system of record behind everything else.' },
  { name: 'Health & wellness', text: 'Coverage and wellness information for members, built into the member app.' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  name="Union Workforce Ecosystem"
  description="Case study: the Union Workforce Ecosystem, a five-portal labor platform Sām Kazemi designed as Lead Engineer at Workforce Systems."
  summary="A five-portal labor workforce platform that unifies dispatch, dues, grievances, training, and benefits into one system of record for union members, contractors, and staff."
  role="Lead Engineer: platform design and delivery"
  org="Workforce Systems (WFS)"
  period="Apr 2026 – Present"
  status="In development under contract"
  live="https://workforcesystems.net/"
  liveLabel="workforcesystems.net"
  accent="lapis"
  stack={['Platform architecture', 'Design systems', 'HTML5 / CSS', 'JavaScript', 'Node.js tooling', 'Responsive and mobile UI', 'Astro']}
>
  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The challenge</p>
    <h2>Unions run on disconnected tools</h2>
    <p>
      A union local keeps its members working through dispatch, collects dues, handles grievances,
      tracks training and certifications, and connects members to benefits. Those jobs usually live in
      separate systems, spreadsheets, and paper, so the same member’s information is scattered and
      often out of sync.
    </p>
    <p>
      Workforce Systems builds software for unions. My job as Lead Engineer is to design the
      platform, build it, and work with union leadership to turn their day-to-day operations into
      a product, including helping secure the contracts that put it to work.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">The platform</p>
    <h2>Five portals, one system of record</h2>
    <p>
      I designed and prototyped a platform where union operations hold the data, and every other
      surface is a view onto it. A contractor’s request for workers becomes a call at the hall, which
      becomes a dispatch in a member’s pocket, with no re-keying in between.
    </p>
    <ul class="cs-flow">
      {
        portals.map((p) => (
          <li>
            <div>
              <strong>{p.name}</strong>
              <span>{p.text}</span>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Engineering highlights</p>
    <h2>Consistency at scale</h2>
    <div class="cs-features">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="shield" /></span>
        <h3>Carrier-agnostic health coverage model</h3>
        <p>
          Normalized insurance data from any carrier into one model with derived totals, used across
          100+ screens, so the numbers members see never disagree from one screen to the next.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="layers" /></span>
        <h3>Shared design system</h3>
        <p>
          One design system across all five portals, from a phone-sized member app to desktop
          operations screens.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="code" /></span>
        <h3>Generator tooling</h3>
        <p>
          Node.js tooling that regenerates shared UI chrome across the screen library, preventing
          drift as the product grows.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="icon-badge"><Icon name="rocket" /></span>
        <h3>From prototype to contract</h3>
        <p>
          Working directly with union leadership so the product reflects how a hall actually runs,
          and helping win the contracts that fund it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="reveal">
    <p class="eyebrow">Why it matters</p>
    <h2>Building for working people</h2>
    <p>
      Laborers deserve software as good as anything in their pockets. When dispatch, dues, and
      benefits live in one place, members spend less time chasing paperwork and more time working,
      and the union can see its whole workforce at a glance. This is early-stage work at a startup,
      and it is the kind of work I want to keep doing: technology in service of people who are
      often overlooked by it.
    </p>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>
